{
  "summary": "* Ambient LLMs: Data-Efficient Supervised Fine-tuning via Temperature Matching introduces Ambient SFT, a framework utilizing a frozen pre-trained model as a noisy teacher to prevent overfitting and catastrophic forgetting during fine-tuning. This approach matches high-temperature conditional distributions to achieve up to a 21% relative improvement over standard SFT across various tasks.\n* StringQFT: A Multiuse Textbook Resource for AI-Assisted Theoretical Physics presents an 18-volume collection with associated benchmarks, retrieval tools, and calculation resources for string constructions in quantum field theory. The resource functions as an infrastructure for AI-assisted theoretical physics by linking textbook derivations to agent-accessible tools.\n* Do LLM Agents Still Need to Be Told How to Self-Improve? studies an autonomous paradigm where agents with a compute budget and tools construct their own self-learning algorithms for context-based optimization. The authors find that capable models successfully self-improve using strategies aligned with cognitive frameworks for self-regulated learning and transfer those learned artifacts to external benchmarks.\n* Higher-Scoring Coding Models Did Not Get More Work Done uses an unexpected export control directive as a natural experiment to study the productivity impact of switching between coding models. By tracking GitHub pull requests, the authors discover that higher benchmark scores did not translate into statistically significant increases in developer productivity or merge rates.\n* Reading Scene States: Uncovering Safety Failures in Vision-Language Models identifies a scene-state safety vulnerability where vision-language models provide harmful assistance by interpreting visual events across different scene states. The paper introduces SceneSafetyBench and the S3TA attack framework to demonstrate that current text-side filters and visual moderation offer limited protection against these vulnerabilities."
}
